Pattern provenance
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every fragment, detection pattern, modifier cue and clause boundary in
data/crisis.patterns.json was written for this package. Each entry names the
sources that informed it (the `sources` field, using the keys below) and its
`relation` to them:

- `informed-by`: the source was consulted for vocabulary or framing; the
  regular expression and its wording are our own.
- `adapted-from`: close to the source's wording, so the source's licence
  terms apply.

In pattern version 0.1.0 every entry is `informed-by`. No entry is
`adapted-from` a source, no source list or term set was copied or converted
into patterns, and no source data files are redistributed with this package.
Entries with an empty `sources` list are original to this package.

Not used in any form: the O'Dea et al. (2015) and Sawhney et al. (2018)
phrase lists, the wording of the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale
(C-SSRS), and the LIWC dictionaries.

Listing a source does not imply that its authors, their institutions or NIMH
endorse this package.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
srl-v1.0  Suicide Risk Lexicon v1.0
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Vocabulary for the suicidal ideation, self-harm, unsafe-with-self, lethal
means, preparation and acute-distress patterns was informed by the construct
definitions and example terms of the Suicide Risk Lexicon v1.0
(construct-tracker, (c) Daniel M. Low and contributors, Apache-2.0).
Low DM, Rankin O, Coppersmith DDL, Bentley KH, Nock MK, Ghosh SS (2026),
J Psychopathol Clin Sci, doi:10.1037/abn0001152.

Source: https://github.com/danielmlow/construct-tracker
Licence of the source: Apache License, Version 2.0
         https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0

The patterns are not copies or adaptations of the lexicon's token lists, and
no lexicon files are included. If a later version adapts lexicon terms
(`relation: "adapted-from"`), it must ship the full Apache-2.0 text and a
change notice (Apache-2.0 section 4) with the package.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
nimh-asq  NIMH Ask Suicide-Screening Questions (ASQ)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Framing informed by the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH) ASQ
Suicide Risk Screening Tool, described by NIMH as a "public domain tool, free
of charge". The ASQ items are second-person questions; the patterns are
first-person statements written for this package.
https://www.nimh.nih.gov/research/research-conducted-at-nimh/asq-toolkit-materials

Source: National Institute of Mental Health. US public domain.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
nimh-warning-signs  NIMH Warning Signs of Suicide
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The immediacy (plan, means, preparation) cues and some acute-distress
patterns were informed by the NIMH publication "Warning Signs of Suicide".
NIMH states the information is in the public domain and may be reused without
permission (images excluded; none are used).
https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/warning-signs-of-suicide

Source: National Institute of Mental Health. US public domain.
